      'CHAPTER 1: THE PHILOSOPHY OF "GRAHA" (THE COSMIC SEIZERS)\nIn Sanskrit, the root word "Grah" means "to grasp, to seize, or to hold captive." Grahas are not merely inert spherical rocks floating through vacuum; they are living cosmic intelligences, focal lenses of the universal mind, and divine functionaries appointed by the Supreme Consciousness to deliver the fruits of our accumulated karma (Prarabdha Karma). When a Graha is active during its Vimshottari Dasha or significant transit, it literally "grasps" human consciousness, altering our emotional perception, neurological chemistry, desires, and decision-making.\n\nThe classical text Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra establishes that the Supreme Divine took the forms of the nine Grahas to sustain the universe and administer cosmic justice. Surya is the soul (Atman), Chandra is the mind (Manas), Mangala is strength and executive vigor (Bala), Budha is speech and discriminative intellect (Vani), Brihaspati is wisdom and divine grace (Jnana), Shukra is reproductive seed and aesthetic devotion (Virya), Shani is sorrow, discipline, and stoic labor (Dukha), while Rahu and Ketu are the poles of material obsession and spiritual liberation.',

